y diagnosis, as they usually present acutely."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Impacted Stool as a Cause of Mechanical Obstruction", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12209302325581399, "posterior": 0.2135593220338983, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Chronic constipation could plausibly cause vomiting via impaction, but the lack of documented impaction makes this a weak association."], "evidence_against": ["The lack of documented impaction reduces the likelihood of impacted stool as the primary explanation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Bowel Volvulus (Twisting of the Bowel)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.012, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The prolonged subacute course strongly argues against bowel volvulus, which typically presents acutely."]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.1", "B1.2", "B2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the patient's chronic constipation explain his vomiting through fecal impaction, given the absence of definitive evidence against this mechanism?", "summary": "Chronic constipation could plausibly cause vomiting via impaction, but the lack of documented impaction makes this a weak association."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 7-day subacute symptom duration argue against adhesions as the primary diagnosis given their typically acute or intermittent presentation?", "summary": "The subacute duration weakens adhesions as the primary diagnosis, as they usually present acutely."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 7-day subacute symptom duration contradict the typical acute presentation of bowel volvulus, which usually requires emergent intervention within hours?", "summary": "The prolonged subacute course strongly argues against bowel volvulus, which typically presents acutely."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a RLQ surgical scar contradict the diagnosis of diabetes-related motility disorders as the primary explanation for vomiting?", "summary": "The RLQ scar is more consistent with mechanical dysfunction, indirectly reducing the likelihood of diabetes-related motility disorders."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's chronic constipation history and absence of documented impaction suggest impacted stool is an unlikely primary cause of his subacute vomiting?", "summary": "The lack of documented impaction reduces the likelihood of impacted stool as the primary explanation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 7-day subacute symptom duration contradict the typical acute presentation of bowel volvulus, which usually requires emergent intervention within hours?", "summary": "The subacute duration of 7 days strongly argues against bowel volvulus (B1.3) due to its typically acute presentation. Adhesions (B1.1) and impacted stool (B1.2) receive moderate support from their respective likelihood ratios, while diabetes-related motility disorders (B2) and chronic constipation…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.457], ["B2", 0.217], ["B1.1", 0.152]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.244], ["B2", 0.174], ["B1.2", 0.122]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.332], ["B1.2", 0.214], ["B2", 0.203]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Bowel volvulus (twisting of the bowel) remains a high-danger unresolved alternative (danger 0.9) despite subacute presentation. Weak lower extremity pulses suggest chronic PAD but do not exclude acute mesenteric ischemia, which must be ruled out urgently.", "required_actions": ["Perform emergent imaging (e.g., abdominal X-ray or CT) to assess for bowel obstruction/volvulus", "Initiate urgent surgical consultation if volvulus is suspected"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Emergency override is suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode, but B1.3 (bowel volvulus) remains a dangerous unresolved alternative (danger 0.9, posterior 0.152). No branch meets posterior >=0.75 for confirmation. Info_exhaustion not triggered as imaging/actions remain unperformed."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2135593220338983, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying etiology of this patient's symptoms?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Adhesions"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Enteric nervous system damage"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Impacted stool"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Norovirus"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Twisting of the bowel"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Visceral angiomatosis', 'Chronic constipation', 'Intestinal ischemia', 'Decreased intestinal transit time', 'Acute constipation'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.457}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.2", "label": "Impacted Stool as a Cause of Mechanical Obstruction", "posterior": 0.214, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The lack of documented impaction reduces the likelihood of impacted stool as the primary explanation."}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B3", "B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nChronic Constipation with Complications vs Peripheral Vascular Disease with GI Complications:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nChronic Constipation with Complications vs Adhesion-related Mechanical Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, blood in stool, dark-colored urine, diarrhea\n\nPeripheral Vascular Disease with GI Complications vs Adhesion-related Mechanical Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal pain, blood in stool, dark-colored urine\n\nRelated to Peripheral Vascular Disease with GI Complications: diabetic peripheral angiopathy, erythromelalgia, intermittent vascular claudication\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.71):]\n  Source: Surgery_Schwartz\n  and motor and sensory abnormalities (e.g., functional dyspepsia). Surgically relevant secondary disorders of gastric motility (e.g., dumping, gastric stasis, and Roux syndrome) are discussed under Pos\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Development and psychometric evaluation of the Diabetic Gastroparesis Symptom Severity Diary. > History and Physical\n  Nausea is the most common symptom in gastroparesis. Other common symptoms include vomiting, early satiety, postprandial fullness, and bloating. Vomitus often contains undigested chewed food. Both weig\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Surgery_Schwartz\n  and motor and sensory abnormalities (e.g., functional dyspepsia). Surgically relevant secondary disorders of gastric motility (e.g., dumping, gastric stasis, and Roux syndrome) are discussed under Pos\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Endoscopic management of gastric outlet obstruction disease. > Treatment / Management\n  Gastroparesis is the most common motility disorder causing GOO. This is often associated with advanced diabetes mellitus but can also be idiopathic. Other causes include medication use (such as opioid\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  nausea and vomiting exacerbated by fatty meals and alcohol → gastroparesis → Diabetes-related Gastrointestinal Motility Disorders\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for gastroparesis (delayed gastric emptying) as a diabetes complication, which could explain meal/alcohol-exacerbated nausea\n  constipation → mechanical intestinal obstruction → Impacted Stool as a Cause of Mechanical Obstruction\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for mechanical intestinal obstruction secondary to impacted stool, given the patient's history of chronic constipation\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates 